    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-counts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Counts as Low Carb</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#isolate-vs-concentrate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Isolate vs Concentrate</a>
          <a href="#sweeteners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sweeteners and Sugar Alcohols</a>
          <a href="#keto" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Is Protein Powder Keto?</a>
          <a href="#mixers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What You Mix It With</a>
          <a href="#who-needs-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Who Needs a Low Carb Powder</a>
          <a href="#keto-branded" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Keto-Branded Powders</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most good protein powders are already low in carbohydrate. The real differences are a few grams per scoop from lactose, flavorings and fillers, plus whatever you mix the powder into, which often matters more than the powder itself. Count carbs per serving, check how the sweetener is counted, and ignore the word keto on the front of the tub.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Counts as Low Carb in a Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no legal definition of a low carb protein powder, so we use a practical one: 3 g of total carbohydrate or less per serving that delivers at least 20 g of protein. By that standard most whey isolates and many plant isolates qualify easily, while mass gainers, meal replacements and many blended or superfood-loaded powders do not. A gainer can carry 50 g or more of carbohydrate per serving, which is the opposite of what a low carb dieter wants.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cleaner way to compare products is carbohydrate per 25 g of protein, because serving sizes vary widely. A scoop with 25 g of protein and 1 g of carbohydrate is a better low carb tool than one with 20 g of protein and 2 g of carbohydrate, even though both look tiny on paper. Dividing by protein also exposes powders that keep carbs low simply by using a small scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the question of net carbs. Many brands advertise net carbs, usually meaning total carbohydrate minus fiber and sugar alcohols. That is a convention, not a regulated number: the FDA does not define net carbs, and brands do not all subtract the same things. A powder with 10 g of total carbohydrate, 5 g of fiber and 4 g of erythritol can advertise 1 g of net carbs. That may be a fair reading for erythritol, but it is not the same thing as a powder with 1 g of total carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical carbs per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Low carb fit</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey concentrate or blend</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3-5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good, if counted</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single-ingredient pea isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1-2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetened plant blend</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-15 g total, often fiber and erythritol</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check net carb math</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mass gainer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our advice is to track total carbohydrate unless you understand exactly what has been subtracted. On a strict ketogenic diet, where a whole day's budget may be 20 to 50 g, the difference between 1 g and 5 g per shake adds up if you drink two shakes a day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Carbs per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, taste panels or a keto trial for this guide. The picks are based on label analysis, published third-party certifications and the research on carbohydrate restriction and protein, the same method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are reporting what buyers widely say rather than claiming results of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The main metric was total carbohydrate per serving relative to protein. Every pick lists 3 g or less per serving on the label, and the top picks list about 1 g or less for roughly 25 g of protein. We also looked at sugar specifically, because a gram of carbohydrate from lactose or added sugar behaves differently from a gram of fiber, and at how each brand counts its sweetener, since some low carb claims depend on subtracting sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond carbs, each product needed a fully disclosed formula without proprietary blends, a meaningful protein dose of 20 g or more, and a long track record with consistent buyer feedback on mixing and taste. We gave credit for independent certification such as Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport, and for brands that publish their own third-party lab results. Every product also had to be currently sold on Amazon in the United States.
          </p>

          <p>
            We deliberately did not reward keto-specific add-ins such as MCT oil powder, exogenous ketones or collagen blends marketed for ketosis. Those ingredients change the price more than the outcome, as we explain further down. If you are cutting calories as well as carbs, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">best protein powder for weight loss</a> guide ranks powders by protein per calorie, and several of the same isolates appear on both lists.
          </p>

